--TEST--
A body declaring `static` may not return the declaring class, since a subclass call site is promised its own (`rule:statements/static-is-a-member-modifier`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `static` is the class the *call* named, so `Leaf::make()` is promised a
// `Leaf`. Both bodies below hand back a `Base` instead, which PHP catches at
// run time with a `TypeError` and Novis has nothing below the type system to
// catch at all — so it is refused at the declaration. `E0741`'s own doc
// comment owns why refusing beats accepting.
class Base {
    public static function make(): static {
        return new self();
    }

    public static function named(): static {
        return new Base();
    }

    // The accepted shapes, kept here so the case says what to write instead:
    // `new static(...)`, `$this`, and a call forwarded through `static::`.
    public static function ok(): static {
        return new static();
    }

    public function chain(): static {
        return $this;
    }

    public static function forwarded(): static {
        return static::ok();
    }
}

class Leaf extends Base {
}

echo "unreachable\n";
// covers: lang:classes/methods-self-static-and-parent
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0741]: a body declaring `static` returns a value that is not the called class
  --> %s:8:36
  |
8 |     public static function make(): static {
  |                                    ------ declared `static` here
9 |         return new self();
  |                ^^^^^^^^^^ not the called class
  = help: `static` is the class the call named, which a subclass may be — return `$this` or `new static(...)`, or declare `self` if the declaring class is what is meant

error[E0741]: a body declaring `static` returns a value that is not the called class
   --> %s:12:37
   |
12 |     public static function named(): static {
   |                                     ------ declared `static` here
13 |         return new Base();
   |                ^^^^^^^^^^ not the called class
   = help: `static` is the class the call named, which a subclass may be — return `$this` or `new static(...)`, or declare `self` if the declaring class is what is meant

error: aborting due to 2 errors
